{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011Cgf4juUANJR75nyE8Rarz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The gate passes now. Nothing was committed.\n\n**Cause.** The hang was not a Lean failure and not the fork-from-threads hazard fix attempt 1 chased. `paper1/false_refutation_hunt.py`'s `run` installs SIGTERM and SIGINT handlers that only set a flag, and never restores them. `test_stop_and_resume` calls `run` twice, so every later test in the suite runs with that handler in place, and every forked `multiprocessing.Pool` worker inherits it. `Pool.terminate()` ends its workers with SIGTERM, because its `_help_stuff_finish` keeps the task queue's reader lock and the exit sentinel cannot reach a worker still waiting for that lock. A worker with the inherited Python-level handler absorbs the signal and goes back to waiting, so `Pool.__exit__` joins it forever. That is exactly the stack in the failing run, inside `run_pebbling_gu`.\n\n**Evidence.**\n- A pytest plugin recording the SIGTERM handler after each test shows the first non-default handler at `test_stop_and_resume` and the same `run.<locals>.on_term` on all 774 tests after it. After the fix, none.\n- The orphaned worker from fix attempt 1's diagnostic run was still alive on the machine: one thread, asleep on a futex, SIGTERM in its caught-signal mask. It survived a plain `kill` and died on SIGKILL. I killed it.\n- The hang is intermittent because the SIGTERM only matters for a worker that is still waiting on the reader lock at exit. The handler turns that race into a permanent wait.\n\n**Fix, in two files.**\n- `paper1/false_refutation_hunt.py`: `run` records the previous handlers and restores them in a `finally` around the rest of the function. The diff looks large because of the re-indent; no logic changed.\n- `tests/test_false_refutation_hunt.py`: `test_stop_and_resume` now asserts the SIGTERM and SIGINT handlers are unchanged after `run`.\n\nFix attempt 1's `tests/conftest.py` and the `gate.py` changes stand. They are what produced the stack dump that pointed at the pool exit.\n\n**Gate result.** lake build ok, 0 sorry, 0 axiom. The main suite passed in 4:24 with 1,816 tests, the pathwidth solver suite in 6:23, and the paper builds at 77 pages with no undefined references. PROGRESS.md has an entry for both fix attempts, the cause, and the gate line.\n\n**One thing recorded but not fixed.** Fix attempt 1's log shows `test_stop_and_resume` failing once with `KeyError: 'tested'` under heavy machine load. The spawned hunt worker had not reported stats within the test's 8-second deadline. It is a load-dependent flake of that test's budget, noted in PROGRESS.md for the owner."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":964,"cache_read_input_tokens":145807,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":964,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":766,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"47b4f29b-53e3-4075-922d-abd3a15e6ae7","uuid":"4e37a37d-a261-41f4-a66c-8c4717f6312b","timestamp":"2026-11-03T00:41:42.889Z","request_id":"req_011Cgf4ju7LAU9bXnYdBcNQe"}
